  "query_preview": "..\n\nA weakly forced cold front lean on moisture is the weather highlight \nfor SE Mi today. The initial wind shift passes through the Tri \nCities and northern Thumb leading up to sunrise while any remaining \npockets of rain continue dissipating. The front lacks moisture and \nis just too far south of the Ontario mid level trough and upper jet \nto sustain precipitation, however clouds are plentiful along and \nbehind the surface boundary. This helps sharpen an already \nrespectable boundary layer temperature gradient, but which takes \nnearly the entire day to reach the Ohio border. The weak and slow \nlow level cold advection allows another day of above normal high \ntemperatures south of I-69, and especially from metro Detroit to the \nOhio border where highs around 70 are once again in reach. Boundary \nlayer cold advection then gains traction tonight as the surface \nfront settles into the Ohio valley and high pressure slides across \nnorthern Ontario to strengthen NE flow into the central Great Lakes. \nLow to mid level frontal clouds linger overhead with the chance for \na stratocu component inland from Lake Huron. Guidance temperatures \nstill only drop to normal lows in the upper 30s Tri Cities and Thumb \nto mid 40s metro Detroit southward by sunrise. \n\nTuesday ends up being cooler compared to recent days, however still \nat or slightly above normal. Mid level clouds hold through the day \nwithin the mid levels of the frontal zone stalled overhead while the \nsurface boundary remains located across the Ohio valley. The clouds \ncombine with steady NE wind into Lower Mi to produce high \ntemperatures in the mid 50s to lower 60s. Broad low pressure \ndeveloping from the central Plains into central Canada then begins a \nnew warming trend across the Midwest which spreads into the Great \nLakes Wednesday. There are additional clouds but still not much sign \nof rain in the warm advection pattern among the 26/00Z models, \nhowever guidance temperatures quickly return to around 70 Wednesday \nafternoon and lower to mid 70s Thursday. Records are in the upper \n70s for Thursday/29th as recently as 2024.   \n\nThe larger scale upper air pattern maintains a split flow in the \nnorthern stream jet during the late week which directs surface low \ntracks from the SW States into central Canada and then eastward. \nThis keeps the associated cold fronts more benign upon reaching \nLower Mi with the next one projected for Thursday night into Friday. \nIt presents the next chance of rain but not much of a temperature \ndrop by Friday. NBM guidance high temperatures hold in the mid to \nupper 60s through the weekend, ahead of these low tracks and in SW \nflow off high pressure pinned near the mid Atlantic coast.",
